    "3.3": `<p>Under Table 5.3 the professor wrote the procedure to follow for every chair question. It has three steps:</p>
<ol><li><b>Draw chairs.</b> Both of them.</li><li><b>Determine which, if any, is more stable.</b></li><li><b>Compare chairs → relationship:</b> same? diastereomers? enantiomers?</li></ol>
<p>Here it is, worked once on cis-1-ethyl-2-methylcyclohexane, with both groups on wedges:</p>
[[ring:1u:CH3,2u:CH2CH3]]
<p><b>Step 1.</b> Both groups are up. On neighbouring carbons, one up position is axial and the other is equatorial. Draw one chair, then flip it. Each group changes between axial and equatorial, and it stays up, because a ring flip never changes up and down.</p>
[[chair:1u:CH3,2u:CH2CH3|A]]
[[chair:1u:CH3,2u:CH2CH3|B]]
<p><b>Step 2.</b> The first chair has methyl axial (cost 1.74) and ethyl equatorial. The second has ethyl axial (cost 1.79). The first chair, with the larger group equatorial, is more stable, by a small margin.</p>
<p><b>Step 3.</b> The chairs have different energies, so they cannot be the same and cannot be mirror images. They are <b>diastereomers</b>: stereoisomers that are not mirror images.</p>
<table><tr><th>Relationship</th><th>Test</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td>same</td><td>the chairs can be superimposed</td><td>cis-1,4-dimethylcyclohexane</td></tr><tr><td>enantiomers</td><td>non-superimposable mirror images, equal energy</td><td>cis-1,2-dimethylcyclohexane</td></tr><tr><td>diastereomers</td><td>neither of the above, usually different energy</td><td>methylcyclohexane</td></tr></table>
<p>The words in the notes, "which, if any", are a reminder that sometimes neither chair is more stable.</p>
<p>Step 3 has a shortcut. Two structures that are identical have the same energy. Two enantiomers also have the same energy, because a mirror does not change any distance inside a molecule. So if step 2 finds that the two chairs have different energies, the relationship must be diastereomers, and step 3 is finished. Only when the energies are equal is there a real choice between same and enantiomers. Then you draw the mirror image of chair 1 and see whether chair 2 matches it, or you try to turn chair 2 in space until it lies on chair 1. The next passage does this for cis-1,2-dimethylcyclohexane.</p>
<p>The flip needs care on paper. Number the carbons on the flat drawing and on both chairs, in the same direction, and mark each group u or d from its wedge or dash. Put each group on its carbon in the first chair and read whether the position is axial or equatorial. For the second chair, every group keeps its u or d and swaps axial for equatorial. Then check that the second chair agrees with the flat drawing. Most lost points in chair questions come from a group that changed face during the flip.</p>
<p>The same three steps are the structure of Question 8 on the past exam, which is worth 100 points. It asks for them for three molecules. Each piece of the answer earns points, and the relationship is the piece most often left out, so writing the three steps as headings helps.</p>
<p>Why draw both chairs? A ring does not stay in one chair. It flips back and forth, so a real sample is a mixture of the two chairs. If one chair has lower energy, more of the molecules are in that chair at any moment, which is why step 2 matters. The table of relationships shows the three answers for step 3. The chairs may be the same, like cis-1,4-dimethylcyclohexane. They may be non-superimposable mirror images with equal energy, like cis-1,2-dimethylcyclohexane. Or they may be diastereomers, like methylcyclohexane, which usually have different energy. Each step earns points on the exam, so write all three, even when the answer to step 2 is "neither", and do not forget the last step.</p>`,
    "4.1": `<p>For any substituted cyclohexane, <b>draw chairs</b>, find which is more stable (<b>Determine which, if any, is more stable.</b>), and then <b>Compare chairs → relationship:</b> chairs with different energies cannot be the same or enantiomers, so they are <b>diastereomers</b>.</p>`,
